            <ArticleTitle>Palpation of the lateral pterygoid area in the myofascial pain diagnosis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e61-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.tripleo.2007.11.004</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To evaluate the diagnostic value of intraoral palpation at the lateral pterygoid (LP) area as part of the physical examination to detect myofascial pain, according to modified research diagnostic criteria for temporomandibular disorders.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Forty-four women composed the myofascial pain group, and 33 symptom-free age-matched were the control group. One examiner calibrated and blinded to group distribution performed 2 intraoral bilateral palpations of the lateral pterygoid.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The LP area palpation showed sensitivity and specificity values of 79.55% and 77.27%, respectively, and positive and negative likelihood ratios of 3.50 and 0.26, respectively.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Palpation at the LP area did not reach acceptable values of specificity, and care must be taken when judging positive response to this procedure.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
